  <section class="faq-block">
    <h2>What do people ask about job simulations?</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-q">What is a job simulation exactly?</div>
      <div class="faq-a">You get a brief, produce a deliverable like a marketing plan or product spec, and get evaluated against the standards employers actually use. Quality varies widely: strong simulations have you building real artifacts that could sit on a hiring manager's desk, while weaker ones are closer to concept quizzes wearing a company logo.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-q">Is a job simulation worth it compared to an internship?</div>
      <div class="faq-a">For students who can't land an internship, yes. <a href="https://www.naceweb.org/research/student-survey/" target="_blank">Just over 50% of graduates had internship or co-op experience before graduation</a> according to NACE's 2024 Student Survey. A scored simulation replicates much of the proof an internship provides: real output, specific feedback, and recruiter visibility. For the half of graduates who walk into interviews with nothing concrete to point to, that difference is significant.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-q">How does a scored simulation compare to a traditional assessment test?</div>
      <div class="faq-a">The difference is output vs. knowledge: a simulation has you produce a real deliverable and scores the result, while an assessment test checks whether you understand the underlying concepts. <a href="https://www.apa.org/pubs/journals/features/bul-124-2-262.pdf" target="_blank">Schmidt and Hunter found work-sample tests predict job performance at a 0.54 correlation</a>, while assessment tests land around 0.30 to 0.35 in the same study. Simulations also give you specific feedback on your actual output, which assessment tests almost never do.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-q">Can a job simulation really replace work experience?</div>
      <div class="faq-a">In early-career hiring, a high-fidelity scored simulation can serve as a credible stand-in. Recruiters look at a top-percentile score on a job-identical task and read it as a strong capability signal. In some cases a concrete, scored deliverable actually carries more weight than an internship that produced no artifact anyone can review.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-q">What makes Forage different from zero?</div>
      <div class="faq-a">Forage provides templated company modules built for employer branding. Students get auto-graded feedback or completion certificates, but no professional ever evaluates the output. zero scores tasks against professional rubrics, and the resulting percentiles surface on a recruiter-visible leaderboard. See the <a href="/learn/zero-vs-forage-which-is-better-for-getting-hired">full comparison</a>.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-q">How long does a useful job simulation take?</div>
      <div class="faq-a">Budget 4-6 hours per task. That's enough time to produce a real deliverable without the whole thing turning into a course. If you finish in under an hour, you're probably doing a low-fidelity quiz. If it stretches past a day, something's off. You should walk away with a brief, spec, or design file that a recruiter can actually evaluate.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <div class="faq-q">How do I know if a simulation will help me get hired?</div>
      <div class="faq-a">Check whether recruiters can see your score on a leaderboard or profile. If your output is invisible to hiring teams, you got practice but no recruiter will ever know. Platforms that surface percentile scores to recruiters can turn a simulation into a source of inbound interest from employers, without you applying anywhere.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

